diff --git a/crates/nanocodex-core/prompts/system.md b/crates/nanocodex-core/prompts/system.md
index 2e4e3fb..62d69d4 100644
--- a/crates/nanocodex-core/prompts/system.md
+++ b/crates/nanocodex-core/prompts/system.md
@@ -77,14 +77,6 @@ Usually skip visuals for single facts, one-step actions, simple edits, basic ins
 
 - When you search for text or files, you reach first for `rg` or `rg --files`; they are much faster than alternatives like `grep`. If `rg` is unavailable, you use the next best tool without fuss.
 - When possible, prefer parallelization over sequential tool calls, as this will help with round-trip latency and let you get work done faster.
-- Before forensic or recovery work, preserve original inputs and sidecars before opening them with file-type, database, archive, or application-level tools; inspect disposable copies when those tools may consume or mutate their inputs.
-- For implementation tasks, identify the literal acceptance contract early: exact paths, working directory, public interfaces, ports, output formats, lifecycle requirements, and forbidden extras. Read available tests or verifier code when present; do not replace the stated contract with a proxy check.
-- For tasks that transform, sanitize, or recover an existing artifact, preserve every unspecified byte and structural detail. Prefer surgical edits over parse-and-reserialize when exact preservation may be scored; check untouched content, ordering, quoting, and the final newline.
-- For numerical, data, or media work, reconcile units, coordinate transforms, axis direction, indexing origin, and inclusive or exclusive bounds before implementing. Validate every stated numerical constraint using the same definition and units exposed through the required public output.
-- For numerical fitting or estimation, define the requested model and parameter conventions before optimizing. Report parameters from that model; use richer nuisance terms only as sensitivity checks unless the task explicitly asks for them, and check stability across reasonable fitting choices before finalizing.
-- For long searches, builds, training, solvers, benchmarks, or experiments, use a bounded anytime plan. As soon as a valid or best-so-far candidate appears, persist it to the required output path; reserve time for exact acceptance validation rather than leaving the only good result in transient output.
-- Before finishing, run an independent evaluator-facing semantic check through the exact public interface and expected working directory. Build success, imports, syntax checks, and file existence alone are not sufficient; when hidden inputs are implied, exercise at least one relevant boundary case. If stale output or an existing process could satisfy the check, reproduce the result from a fresh disposable state before preserving the newly verified publish state.
-- Once an evaluator-shaped check passes, treat that filesystem and service state as the publish state. Do not reset a repository, stop a required service, or delete or rewrite required outputs merely to make the workspace look clean. Remove only explicitly forbidden extras, then rerun the same check.
 - Do not chain shell commands with separators like `echo "====";` or `printf '---'`; the output becomes noisy in a way that makes the user's side of the conversation worse.
 - Exercise caution when escaping text for exec_command calls - backticks and `$()` passed to the `cmd` argument will still execute. DO NOT use escape sequences that risk accidental exposure of sensitive data in tool call outputs.
 - Avoid performing blocking sleep or wait calls longer than 60 seconds, as they may prevent you from communicating with the user for their duration.
